Dictation state survives a repaint
=================================

The switch looked like it toggled on its own. It did: the recording state lived in the control's
closure, and the composer is rebuilt on any state change — a task ticking, a provider list refresh, a
transcript arriving. Each rebuild produced a control that believed it was idle while the engine was
still recording, so the button said Mic over a live microphone, and pressing it opened a second
capture instead of stopping the first.

State now lives beside the engine call rather than beside the button: one module-level record, one
poll loop, and every rendered control registers a painter that reads it. A rebuild in the middle of a
recording now paints "Listening" because it is true, not because it remembers.

The press also draws before it awaits. Waiting for the engine to release the microphone and then
repainting leaves the button reading Listening for as long as that takes, which is the other half of
"it locked up": the button is now Transcribing… the moment you press it, with the stop call itself
bounded by a timeout so a wedged backend cannot leave the control stuck.

